@meta
id: 08-05
title: When one character becomes a word
chapter: 08
type: lesson
skills: substitute-s
par: 52
minutes: 5
vscode: double-click the letter and type the word
open: seed.ts

@brief
`r` swaps one character for one character. When the replacement is longer
than what it replaces, `r` is no use: you would have to delete, then find
your way into insert mode at the right spot.

There is a key that fuses those two steps. It removes the character under
the cursor and drops you straight into insert mode where it was.

The seed data still uses the old single-letter statuses. The enum now
spells them out: `P` is `PENDING`, `S` is `SHIPPED`, `C` is `CANCELLED`.
The quotes stay exactly where they are.

@keys
| `s` | delete the character under the cursor and start inserting |
| `$` | end of the line |
| `3h` | three characters left |
| `Esc` | back to normal mode |

@hints
You need the character gone *and* the cursor in its place, ready to type.
One key does both.
--
`s` is `x` and `i` in a single press. Type the word, then `Esc`.
--
The letter is three characters back from the end of each line: `$` then
`3h` lands on it. Then `s`, the word, `Esc`, and `j` to the next one.

@solution
    j $3h sPENDING<Esc>  j $3h sSHIPPED<Esc>  j $3h sCANCELLED<Esc>  :wq<Enter>

Most of that count is the words themselves, which you would have typed
whatever editor you were in. The vim part is four keystrokes a line.

`s` is worth knowing by its real name: *substitute*. It is the first key
in this chapter that leaves you in insert mode, and that changes the
rhythm — every `s` needs an `Esc` to finish it, in a way that `x` and `r`
never do.

There is a linewise version, `S`, which throws the whole line away and
leaves you inserting on an empty line with the indentation kept. You will
meet it again in a few lessons under a different name, because vim gives
that exact command two spellings.

@start seed.ts
export async function seed() {
  await seedOrder('ord_1042', 'P');
  await seedOrder('ord_1043', 'S');
  await seedOrder('ord_1044', 'C');
}

@expected seed.ts
export async function seed() {
  await seedOrder('ord_1042', 'PENDING');
  await seedOrder('ord_1043', 'SHIPPED');
  await seedOrder('ord_1044', 'CANCELLED');
}
